  "preamble": "Assibilation of [d] to [dz]: the stop develops a fricative release, usually before high vowels. In Late Middle Japanese /du/ was pronounced [dzu] (Old Japanese midu 'water' > midzu, later mizu), in Quebec French /d/ is regularly affricated to [dz] before /i y j ɥ/ (difficile [dzifisɪl]), and in Swati and Phuthi Proto-Nguni *d became dz (Proto-Nguni *-de 'long' > Swati -dze, while Zulu and Xhosa keep -de).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Assibilation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Japanese (Late Middle Japanese)",
      "languageFamily": "Japonic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Japanese みづ mi₁du 'water'",
          "to": "[midzu] (now mizu [mizɯ])",
          "note": "/t/ and /d/ were affricated before /u/ (and /i/); [dzu] later merged with [zu] in most dialects (the yotsugana merger)."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Japanese 鼓 tudumi 'hand drum'",
          "to": "[tsudzumi] (now tsuzumi)",
          "note": "Both stops are affricated in the same word: /tudumi/ > /t͡sud͡zumi/ > /t͡suzumi/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
